First, let's find an expression for $\dfrac{d}{dx}[-2f(x)+3g(x)-2]$ in terms of $f'$ and $g'$:

$\begin{align}
&\phantom{=}\dfrac{d}{dx}[-2f(x)+3g(x)-2]
\\\\
&=\dfrac{d}{dx}[-2f(x)]+\dfrac{d}{dx}[3g(x)]-\dfrac{d}{dx}(2)&&\gray{\text{Sum and difference rules}}
\\\\
&=-2f'(x)+3g'(x)-0&&\gray{\text{Constant and constant multiple rules}}
\end{align}$

So we found that $\dfrac{d}{dx}[-2f(x)+3g(x)-2]=-2f'(x)+3g'(x)$.
